</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Proof Sketch of Formal Consequences of Structural Properties of MASS</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we elaborate on the formal consequences of the structural properties of MASS using proof sketches.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Proposition 1: Heterogeneity Non-Reducibility</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}=\{f,g,\bar{\theta}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}=\{f,g,\bar{\theta}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a homogeneous population where all agents are drawn i.i.d. from a common distribution <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}\sim\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}\sim\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, with no systematic archetype differentiation. Let <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}=\{f_{i},g_{i},\theta_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}=\{f_{i},g_{i},\theta_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a heterogeneous population with <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[\theta_{i}]=\bar{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[\theta_{i}]=\bar{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> structured archetype composition, i.e. a mixture of <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> distinct agent types, such that the marginal statistics of <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are identical to those of <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. (i) There exists a formulation <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g,G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g,G)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> produce different population-level outcomes <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. (ii) Further, let there be a <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> which shares identical archetype distributions over <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> but different structural placement in <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. There exist <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g,G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g,G)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> produce different <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(t)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Proof Sketch</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(i) Heterogeneity vs. homogeneity. Under <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, all agents emit messages of uniform intensity, producing similar influence across <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. In <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
, the <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> amplifier agents with <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{amp}}\gg\bar{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>amp</mtext></msub><mo>≫</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{amp}}\gg\bar{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> emits disproportionally high-intensity messages. This produces asymmetric influence cascades regardless of their position in <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is non-trivial, the asymmetric message intensities produce different state trajectories <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{i}(t)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{i}(t)\}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> than under <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This happens, even when <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[\theta_{i}]=\bar{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[\theta_{i}]=\bar{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> for both populations. Therefore, the divergence of population-level outcomes <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Phi_{P_{1}}(t)-\Phi_{P_{2}}(t)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Phi_{P_{1}}(t)-\Phi_{P_{2}}(t)|</annotation></semantics></math> grows for all <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(ii) Placement within G. Let <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a scale-free network with a degree distribution following a power-law. This is characteristic of real social media platforms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Partition <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> into two sets: <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> are the hub nodes, where <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=\{i\in V:deg(i)&gt;&gt;deg\_mean\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=\{i\in V:deg(i)&gt;&gt;deg\_mean\}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> are the peripheral nodes where <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\{i\in V:deg(i)\approx 1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\{i\in V:deg(i)\approx 1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. In <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, assign <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> amplifier agents to <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. in <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, place the same <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> amplifier agents uniformly at random across <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. Amplifier agents emit messages with a gain of 1.5, i.e. <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}(t)=clip(\gamma\cdot x_{i}(t),-1,1),\gamma=1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}(t)=clip(\gamma\cdot x_{i}(t),-1,1),\gamma=1.5</annotation></semantics></math>.
By construction, <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> have identical mean and variance of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, and only differ in where the <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> amplifier agents are structurally located in <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. The expected reach of messages from amplifiers in <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> have a <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="deg\_hub=|\{k:i\in N(k)\}|=deg(i)&gt;deg\_mean" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">deg\_hub=|\{k:i\in N(k)\}|=deg(i)&gt;deg\_mean</annotation></semantics></math> and amplifiers in <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> have <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[deg(i)]=deg\_mean" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[deg(i)]=deg\_mean</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(t+1)=h(G(t),\{m_{i}(t)\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(t+1)=h(G(t),\{m_{i}(t)\})</annotation></semantics></math>, the higher reach of messages generated by hub agents in <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> will reshape <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to further reinforce their structural advantage through preferential attachment, while <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> has no such convergence because of the random placement. The divergence of population-level outcomes <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Phi_{P_{2}}(t)-\Phi_{P_{2}^{\prime}}(t)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Phi_{P_{2}}(t)-\Phi_{P_{2}^{\prime}}(t)|</annotation></semantics></math> therefore grows for all <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, even though <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> have identical aggregate <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> statistics. <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Consequence</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, a homogeneous population will produce categorically different population-level effects as a heterogeneous population.
Second, agent archetypes are not exchangeable units. For example, the same capability concentrated at a hub produces categorically different population-level effects than if it were concentrated at the periphery. Strategic heterogeneity cannot be characterized using aggregate statistics, so one needs to study the joint distribution of archetypes and network positions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Proposition 2 (Local Observability Constraint).</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let two MASS instances <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=(f,g,G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=(f,g,G)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}=(f,g,G^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}=(f,g,G^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> share identical agent states <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{i}(t)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{i}(t)\}</annotation></semantics></math> and identical global aggregate information. That is, the population-level summary statistics <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(t)=\Psi^{\prime}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(t)=\Psi^{\prime}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> over all agent states, but the two instances would differ in the network structure (i.e., <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\neq G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\neq G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>). Then, there exists <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math> such that the population trajectories <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\{x_{i}(t+k)\},G(t+k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\{x_{i}(t+k)\},G(t+k)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\{x^{\prime}_{i}(t+k)\},G^{\prime}(t+k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\{x^{\prime}_{i}(t+k)\},G^{\prime}(t+k)</annotation></semantics></math> diverge for all <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Proof sketch</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since agent <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> observe only messages from its neighborhood, <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}(t)=\{m_{j}(t)|j\in N(i)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}(t)=\{m_{j}(t)|j\in N(i)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, its state update <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}(t+1)=g(x_{i}(t),M_{i}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}(t+1)=g(x_{i}(t),M_{i}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> is conditioned on the local neighborhood not the global aggregate <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\neq G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\neq G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exists at least one agent <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}(i)\neq N_{G^{\prime}}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}(i)\neq N_{G^{\prime}}(i)</annotation></semantics></math>. Even though <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{i}(t)\}=\{x_{i}^{\prime}(t)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{i}(t)\}=\{x_{i}^{\prime}(t)\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(t)=\Psi^{\prime}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(t)=\Psi^{\prime}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> by the setup assumption, but because the same global agent states have different neighborhoods <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}(t)\neq M_{i}^{\prime}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}(t)\neq M_{i}^{\prime}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since there exists at least one pair <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i}(t),M_{i}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i}(t),M_{i}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x_{i}(t),M_{i}(t))\neq x_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x_{i}(t),M_{i}(t))\neq x_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, that means that <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is non-trivial, so <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}(t)\neq M_{i}^{\prime}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}(t)\neq M_{i}^{\prime}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> imples <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}(t+1)\neq x_{i}^{\prime}(t+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}(t+1)\neq x_{i}^{\prime}(t+1)</annotation></semantics></math> for at least one agent <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. This divergence in agent states at <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math> produces divergent messages where <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}(t+1)\neq m_{i}^{\prime}(t+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}(t+1)\neq m_{i}^{\prime}(t+1)</annotation></semantics></math> through the information function <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. Because the input messages to <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are different, <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(t+2)=h((G(t+1),\{m_{i}(t+1)\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(t+2)=h((G(t+1),\{m_{i}(t+1)\})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}(t+2)=h(G^{\prime}(t+1),\{m_{i}^{\prime}(t+1)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}(t+2)=h(G^{\prime}(t+1),\{m_{i}^{\prime}(t+1)\}</annotation></semantics></math>) diverge.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, for any <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the joint trajectory of <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x(t+k),G(t+k))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x(t+k),G(t+k))</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> differs from the joint trajectory <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{\prime}(t+k),G^{\prime}(t+k))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{\prime}(t+k),G^{\prime}(t+k))</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. So the population-level outcome differs, i.e. <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(t+k)\neq\Phi^{\prime}(t+k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(t+k)\neq\Phi^{\prime}(t+k)</annotation></semantics></math>. This divergence happens despite <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(t)=\Psi^{\prime}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(t)=\Psi^{\prime}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, establishing that global aggregate information is insufficient to determine the system trajectory. Therefore, <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible determinant of population-level outcomes. <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Consequence</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Models and governance mechanisms that operate on aggregate population-level statistics are insufficient to predict MASS dynamics. This is because two systems that are identical at the aggregate level but are networked differently will diverge. The network structure must be treated as a variable in both the modeling and evaluation stages, and not glossed over.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Proposition 3 (Co-evolutionary sensitivity).</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=(f,g,G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=(f,g,G)</annotation></semantics></math> be a MASS and <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> be an instance identical to <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> except that there is a perturbation <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> introduced at time <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, there is a bounded shift <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta x_{i}(t),|\delta x_{i}(t)|=\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta x_{i}(t),|\delta x_{i}(t)|=\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> in the state of a single agent <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, there exists <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g,G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g,G)</annotation></semantics></math> such that the divergence in population-level outcomes <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Phi(t+k)-\Phi_{\epsilon}(t+k)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Phi(t+k)-\Phi_{\epsilon}(t+k)|</annotation></semantics></math> diverges superlinearly in <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, even as <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Proof sketch</h4>

<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let agent <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> be in a hub position at <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and its state perturbation be defined as: when <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> differ from <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\delta x_{i}(t)|=\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\delta x_{i}(t)|=\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. The perturbed agent <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> will then emit a shifted message <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}\epsilon(t)=f(x_{i}(t)+\delta x_{i}(t),\theta_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}\epsilon(t)=f(x_{i}(t)+\delta x_{i}(t),\theta_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. This message differs from its unperturbed message <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta m_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta m_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, which will be bounded below by a function of <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The perturbed message will propagate to all <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in N(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in N(k)</annotation></semantics></math>. For a hub agent, <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|N(i)|=deg(i)&gt;deg\_mean" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|N(i)|=deg(i)&gt;deg\_mean</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. the degree of a hub agent is higher than that of the mean degree of the network), so the perturbation reaches a large fraction of <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Each agent in <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> that is reached will update its state as <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k}(t+1)=g(x_{k}(t),\{...,m_{i}\epsilon(t),...\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k}(t+1)=g(x_{k}(t),\{...,m_{i}\epsilon(t),...\})</annotation></semantics></math>. These perturbed states at <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math> will then generate perturbed messages at <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math>, which propagates through their ego-network to neighboring agents, and so forth.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The perturbation causes input messages to differ, that means <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(t+1)=h(G(t),\{m_{i}(t)\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(t+1)=h(G(t),\{m_{i}(t)\})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\epsilon}(t+1)=h(G(t),\{m_{i}\epsilon(t)\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\epsilon}(t+1)=h(G(t),\{m_{i}\epsilon(t)\})</annotation></semantics></math> diverges. The structural divergence at <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math> means that the ego-network at <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+2</annotation></semantics></math> differs, reshaping the agent influence in subsequent rounds. The compound effect of state perturbations propagating through a diverging network structure produces a divergence <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Phi(t+k)-\Phi_{\epsilon}(t+k)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Phi(t+k)-\Phi_{\epsilon}(t+k)|</annotation></semantics></math>, which is not linear in <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Consequence</h4>

<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Co-evolutionary MASS are sensitive to small perturbations unlike single-task agentic systems. Perturbations can involve a single new agent, a shifted edge, a change in agent internal state, or a single anomalous message, to name a few. These perturbations can produce qualitatively different population-level outcomes at scale. The effect of perturbations and the system’s resilience to perturbation cannot be evaluated at the individual agent level, and governance has to consider system-wide behavioral shifts even when implementing local interventions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Proposition 4 (Non-existence of a Stationary Distribution).</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=(f,g,G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=(f,g,G)</annotation></semantics></math> be a MASS. Then there exits no distribution <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> that is both stationary (i.e., <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{t}(x)=p*(x)\forall t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)=p*(x)\forall t</annotation></semantics></math>) and independent of the system’s interaction dynamics.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Proof sketch</h4>

<div id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that a stationary distribution <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p*(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p*(x)</annotation></semantics></math> independent of the system dynamics exist, such that <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)=p*(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)=p*(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)=p(x|G(t),\{x_{i}(t)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)=p(x|G(t),\{x_{i}(t)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, a stationary distribution requires:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(x|G(t),\{x_{i}(t)\})=p*(x)\forall t,\forall G(t),\forall\{x_{i}(t)\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo>∀</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x|G(t),\{x_{i}(t)\})=p*(x)\forall t,\forall G(t),\forall\{x_{i}(t)\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For this to hold, <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> must be independent of the conditioning on <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{i}(t)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{i}(t)\}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. But this cannot hold under non-trivial <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. By assumption, <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is non-trivial, because there exists at least one pair <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i}(t),M_{i}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i}(t),M_{i}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x_{i}(t),M_{i}(t))\neq x_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x_{i}(t),M_{i}(t))\neq x_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, which means that agent states evolve as a function of the influence of the messages from the neighborhood. Therefore, <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{i}(t)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{i}(t)\}</annotation></semantics></math> is not constant in <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)=p(x|G(t),\{x_{i}(t)\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)=p(x|G(t),\{x_{i}(t)\})</annotation></semantics></math> varies with <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> through the conditioning on <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{i}(t)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{i}(t)\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Also, by assumption <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is non-trivial, because there exists at least one configuration <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G(t),\{m_{i}(t)\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G(t),\{m_{i}(t)\})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(G(t),\{m_{i}(t)\})\neq G(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(G(t),\{m_{i}(t)\})\neq G(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, which means <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> evolves as a function of agent behavior. So <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is not constant in <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> varies with <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> because of its conditioning on <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(t)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> varies with <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> through both <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{i}(t)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{i}(t)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and both quantities themselves are functions of the interaction trajectory of <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be determined by the current system state of <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, but requires knowledge of the full interaction history <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{G(0),G(1),...,G(t)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{G(0),G(1),...,G(t)\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\{x_{i}(0)\},\{x_{i}(1)\},...,\{x_{i}(t)\}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\{x_{i}(0)\},\{x_{i}(1)\},...,\{x_{i}(t)\}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
So therefore, <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be equal any fixed <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p*(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p*(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the contradiction of the assumption of a stationary distribution, there is therefore no stationary distribution <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p*(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p*(x)</annotation></semantics></math> that is independent of the system dynamics that exists. <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Consequence</h4>

<div id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is no fixed ground-truth distribution from which MASS data is drawn against and which agents can be evaluated. Evaluation methods must track <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> as a time-varying system variable using instruments that adapt to the evolving population.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Instantiations of Multi-Agent Social Systems</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Multi-Agent LLM Systems</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In multi-agent LLM systems, the agents population is primarily or exclusively machine. This is the instantiation that is most proximal to current agentic AI practice. <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is the communication topology among LLM-powered agents, often in hierarchical task-oriented orchestrator-worker frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, or peer-to-peer frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> consists of structured natural language messages that are passed between agents. <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> captures an agent’s role, persona, working memory and the current task context.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The information exchange map <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is typically an LLM conditioned on the agent’s persona and the task context. The influence update map <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> captures memory updates, plan revisions and reflection loops triggered by the message exchange.
Current multi-agent LLM systems are still task-bounded: CAMEL pairs agents through role playing dialogue <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, and multi-agent systems debate with each other to improve factuality and reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, and so forth. In all these cases, the agents are a mechanism for a task, and the social group they form are not an object of study.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As multi-agent LLM systems move towards more persistent and open deployments, MASS dynamics begin to surface. LLM agents populating a simulated town produce emergent social behaviors like gossip spreading <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>, norm formation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> or spontaneous coordination <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">99</a>]</cite>, that arise from the interaction structure <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> rather than any individual agent’s design. In such deployments, the MASS structural properties begin to surface: (1) Distributional instability is directly observable as the distribution of generated text evolves because the generation is conditioned on the agent’s past or other agents inputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>. (2) Co-evolution is present when the agent’s memory updates, reshaping the effective context that the future messages are interpreted against <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite>. (3) Strategic heterogeneity emerges as agents develop differentiated roles, whether through apriori preconditioning or through emergent persona drift <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, each with distinct parameterizations of <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g,\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g,\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>. (4) Network-constrained dependence is built into the architecture: in hierarchical systems, worker agents observe only messages from their orchestrator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>; in conversational systems, agents only observe outputs of their interlocutors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The MASS framing reveals what current evaluation of multi-agent LLM systems misses. Current evaluation (i.e., task completion rates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, code correctness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>, accuracy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>) measures the output of the system pipeline, but not the dynamics of the group. Population-level quantities like epistemic diversity across agents or narrative drift over extended interactions require measurement in the MASS primitives, which can be grounded in social science traditions that have studied these phenomena in human population.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Other Instantiations</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The MASS framework can also be extended to other domains where the same structural logic applies. In open-world video games and synthetic social environments, populations of agents interact in the created worlds, producing social norms, economic inequality and collective behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">101</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>MoltBook as an example of MASS</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MoltBook is an online social media platform populated entirely by LLM agents. We used a data sample of consisting of more than 2,126,962 posts and replies from 39,700 unique authors dated 31 Jan to 8 Feb 2026 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>. We construct <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> are LLM-agents, <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{u,v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{u,v}</annotation></semantics></math> means that agent <math id="A4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> replied to agent <math id="A4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. The reply interaction is either a comment-to-comment or a comment-to-post interaction. Each agent’s state <math id="A4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is the karma score an agent accumulated as an engagement proxy. We perform four experiments, each tests one structural prior prediction against a null hypothesis <math id="A4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> based on current agentic AI systems.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset Statistics</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Dataset Statistics ‣ Appendix D MoltBook as an example of MASS ‣ Social Theory Should Be a Structural Prior for Agentic AI: A Formal Framework for Multi-Agent Social Systems" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a> shows the data statistics of our the subset of MoltBook interactions that we analyzed.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For P1 and P2, we partitioned agents by network degree into hub agents (25th- percentile), periphery agents (75th-percentile) and mid agents (the rest of the agents). This yielded groups of 4,571, 8,707, and 4,970 agents respectively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T3" class="ltx_table">
