</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Distinction with Adjacent Framings</h2>

<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multi-agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL)</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The MARL research program studies learned agents interacting under a shared reward structure, typically in simulated environments with well-defined payoffs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a>]</cite>. MASS encompasses both real and simulated ecosystems where the rewards can differ across agents and can also be hidden (i.e., covert agents as often seen in state-sponsored agents on social media <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLM-based Agentic Frameworks</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM-based agentic frameworks like AutoGen and MetaGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">98</a>]</cite> study interacting language agents collaborate on tasks like software engineering workflows and multi-turn problem solving. However, the agents in these systems collaborate under predefined roles and interaction protocols. Rather than focus on such micro-level agent-agent coordination, MASS emphasize the macro-level, where heterogeneous agents interact in an evolving network and result in emergence of population-level phenomena like information diffusion, influence and collective coordination <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Theory-of-Mind (ToM)</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Theory-of-Mind to the capacity of an agent to model the beliefs, intentions and knowledge states of other agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite>. The ToM research program asks whether agent <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> can represent agent <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>’s belief state.
Social theory further understands ToM as socially situated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">88</a>]</cite>: an agent’s model of others is filtered through <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(i)</annotation></semantics></math> and shaped by the structural context of <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. ToM is a component of <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math>, and MASS is the system within which these components operate at scale.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Social simulation</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Generative agent simulation work can model populations with rich agent-agent interaction dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. As such, one might argue that MASS are already well captured within existing simulation frameworks, where agent behavior and interaction can be studied at scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We view social simulation and MASS as complementary rather than equivalent. Social simulation asks: can we model an agent’s behavior realistically within a controlled environment? MASS asks: what happens when agents are deployed in open, real-world environments where they coexist and interact with humans and other agents? While simulation provides a valuable tool for understanding agent behavior, MASS focuses on the dynamics that arise when the model and the world are no longer disparate.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Social cybersecurity</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The social cybersecurity research program has, among others, studied how automated bots accounts shape online discourse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. MASS extends this lineage towards increasingly agentic bot-human systems. The detection, coordination and influence problems that the field has been studying do not disappear, but the study of heuristic-based and scripted bots would give way to understanding populations consisting of adaptive, AI-powered social actors.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Design Principles &amp; Evaluation Benchmarks for MASS</h2>

<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>Design Principles for MASS</h3>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here are some design questions to guide the development of AI systems operating in a multi-agent and interactive environment. These principles are aligned along the four structural properties.</p>
</div>
<section id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">P1. Distributional Instability</h4>

<div id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This property states that the data distribution <math id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is not fixed, and is generated by the system itself. Design questions to consider are:</p>
<ul id="A6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What is the expected distribution or distribution shift of information <math id="A6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> at deployment time?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How do the agents handle feedback loops where their outputs influence their future inputs? The design should consider if the agent should be robust to such feedback loops or should adapt accordingly.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Does the system maintain stability under evolving outputs? If not, does the system have a mechanism to detect when <math id="A6.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> has drifted beyond acceptable bounds? The design should have mechanisms like memory decay or diversity constraints to prevent runaway amplification.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Is your evaluation dataset drawn from the same distribution the system encounters at <math id="A6.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>? The design of the evaluation dataset must be similar to the distribution as the original system, or an analogous distribution.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">P2. Co-evolution</h4>

<div id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This property states that the agents and environment mutually shape each other over time. Design questions to consider are:</p>
<ul id="A6.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A6.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Do agent behaviors generate content, signals or actions that become inputs to other agents or to future versions of itself? If so, what is the feedback loop, and what is the timescale that it operates on?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How do agent updates account for multi-round interaction effects? That is, how does the system adapt in response to the agents behavior? The system can react dynamically, or only under specific conditons.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What happens when perturbations to the system is introduced? These perturbations can be new agents or policy changes.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Is there a mechanism to detect and respond to adversarial adaptation (e.g. agents that exploit the original policy) or cascading feedback loops (e.g. echo-chambers, infinite cascades)?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">P3. Strategic Heterogeneity</h4>

<div id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This property states that agents differ in roles, incentive and influence.</p>
<ul id="A6.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A6.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What types of agents exist? The design must consider the different agents define their behavioral mechanics <math id="A6.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The design must also characterize the functional roles that these archetype play in <math id="A6.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. are they a bridging node, a broadcast node, or a peripheral node?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How is influence distributed across agents? Influence can be distributed equally, or centralized at the hubs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Does the system behave appropriately when interacting with each archetype, or has it only been tested against a homogeneous agent population?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Can a particular archetype exploit the mechanics of <math id="A6.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g,G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g,G)</annotation></semantics></math> that produces harmful population-level outcomes? An amplifier bot that extensively uses star broadcast structures can flood the network <math id="A6.I3.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with undesired narratives, therefore only allowing its narrative to pass through <math id="A6.I3.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">P4. Network-Constrained Dependence</h4>

<div id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This property states that interaction structures determine information and influence flow.</p>
<ul id="A6.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A6.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What is the interaction graph <math id="A6.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>? The interaction network can be defined through an artifact of the agent’s behavior (i.e., replies), as is common with social media and communication studies. It can also be a fixed network that is determined apriori at the system’s initialization.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What does each agent observe? Who can observe whom, and who can influence whom? The design should consider the local vs global context, and what <math id="A6.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(i)</annotation></semantics></math> looks like for the agents.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How does the network topology (i.e., hubs, cluster, bridges, isolates) affect outcomes?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What constraints exist on agent communication and visibility of information?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I4.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I4.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I4.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Are there structural positions in <math id="A6.I4.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> that could give an agent disproportionate influence? The design must consider such types of positions and the agents that are likely to occupy these positions, and how the system is affected by such positional occupancy.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.2 </span>Evaluation Benchmarks</h3>

<div id="A6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We outline example benchmark configurations that can operationalize evaluation under Multi-Agent Social Systems, focusing on population-level dynamics rather than individual task performance. These benchmarks are not intended as exhaustive or definitive, but intended to provide a starting point for the community to deploy and evaluate MASS.</p>
</div>
<section id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Stability Under Interaction</h4>

<div id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This benchmark tests the system’s robustness under distributional instability.</p>
<ul id="A6.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A6.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Setup: Initialize <math id="A6.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> agents with the corresponding diverse initial states <math id="A6.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Place agents in the network <math id="A6.I5.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Run interaction mechanics for <math id="A6.I5.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> rounds.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Measures: distributional drift over time, convergence or divergence of outcomes and agent states <math id="A6.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sensitivity to Perturbation</h4>

<div id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This benchmark tests co-evolution mechanics and system’s robustness or fragility.</p>
<ul id="A6.I6" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A6.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Setup: Initialize <math id="A6.I6.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> agents with the corresponding diverse initial states <math id="A6.I6.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Place agents in the network <math id="A6.I6.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Inject a small number of agents with biased behavior at time <math id="A6.I6.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=k</annotation></semantics></math>. Run interaction mechanics for <math id="A6.I6.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> rounds.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Measures: Shift in population distribution, information cascade size and spread (from <math id="A6.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>), time to recovery to original distribution, if any</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Heterogeneity Stress Test</h4>

<div id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This benchmark tests the effects of strategic heterogeneity of agent archetypes.</p>
<ul id="A6.I7" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A6.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Setup: Initialize <math id="A6.I7.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> agents in a network <math id="A6.I7.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and run interaction mechanics for <math id="A6.I7.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> rounds. Vary the set of <math id="A6.I7.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> agents types: homogeneous vs heterogeneous populations, role-based agents (e.g. amplifiers, coordinators)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Measures: outcome sensitivity to agent composition, role-dependent effects, patterns of information and influence over agent composition</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Network Topology Variation</h4>

<div id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This benchmark tests the effects of network topology on the system.</p>
<ul id="A6.I8" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A6.I8.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I8.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I8.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Setup: Initialize <math id="A6.I8.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> agents in a network <math id="A6.I8.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and run interaction mechanics for <math id="A6.I8.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> rounds. Run the same system under different network topologies of <math id="A6.I8.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, like erdos-renyi, small-world or scale-free graphs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I8.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I8.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I8.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Measures: cascade structure, diffusion speed, local vs global agreement, outcome variability with network topology</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
